1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:05,890
All right, chapter 13, female genital system and gestational pathology.

2
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:09,210
So this is a very important chapter for a few reasons.

3
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:12,790
Number one, you're going to see a ton of the pathology that we discuss clinically.

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:16,670
Of course, this is our major emphasis on the female genital tract,

5
00:00:16,770 --> 00:00:20,550
and many of these lesions are going to arise commonly.

6
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:24,090
And then the other thing I would say is that if you understand the female tract,

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:26,750
the male tract becomes much easier to understand.

8
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:32,030
And so by mastering this particular chapter, the subsequent chapter in male genital

9
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:33,790
will also become much more straightforward.

10
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:39,430
The way I organize this chapter is to take you from the walk along the tract,

11
00:00:39,510 --> 00:00:42,510
from the most distal portion to the more proximal portion.

12
00:00:42,670 --> 00:00:46,090
And so we're going to start with the vulva, and then we'll go to the vaginal canal,

13
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:50,190
and then eventually to the cervix, and then to the endometrium and myometrium, et cetera.

14
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:52,510
That's the way we're going to do this chapter.

15
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:54,610
So let's actually begin with the vulva.

16
00:00:54,610 --> 00:00:59,230
And the vulva, you're well aware, represents the skin and the mucosa that's external to the hymen.

17
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:04,550
So the skin and the mucosa of the female genitalia that's external to the hymen, that's the vulva.

18
00:01:05,190 --> 00:01:10,870
It includes things like the labia majora, labia minora, the vestibule, et cetera.

19
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:14,610
And important to note that it's lined by squamous epithelium,

20
00:01:14,690 --> 00:01:17,630
and that'll come into play a little later in our discussions.

21
00:01:18,270 --> 00:01:22,710
Now the very first lesion that we're going to discuss is called a Bartholin cyst.

22
00:01:22,710 --> 00:01:28,950
And to be honest with you, the only thing that you really need in order to understand this lesion

23
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:31,670
is to go back to review a little bit of normal.

24
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:35,170
So what I'm going to draw for you here, this is the vestibule.

25
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:39,570
The vestibule is sort of the opening to the vaginal canal.

26
00:01:39,570 --> 00:01:44,010
So this central space in this square that I've drawn would represent the vaginal canal.

27
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:49,210
And what you need to remember is that over here, you've sort of got this duct that comes in,

28
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,150
and another one down here at the lower portion of the vestibule.

29
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:58,950
And behind that, underneath that I should say, is a little gland called the Bartholin gland.

30
00:01:58,950 --> 00:02:05,190
And the Bartholin gland secretes mucus-like fluid, which lubricates or lines this vestibule.

31
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:08,110
And basically, that's the normal.

32
00:02:08,270 --> 00:02:11,790
So if you understand the normal, then understanding a Bartholin cyst,

33
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:15,390
which is the pathology that we're discussing, is really no rocket science.

34
00:02:15,790 --> 00:02:18,490
The notion here is that if you obstruct this duct,

35
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:24,210
then what will happen is you'll get a cystic dilatation of the gland behind it.

36
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:28,230
And so this will enlarge, and you'll be able to see on examination,

37
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:32,290
when you're examining the patient, that there's actually an enlarged lesion here.

38
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:33,930
Usually it's unilateral.

39
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:35,950
And so that would be a Bartholin cyst.

40
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:37,930
Now you could ask a few questions.

41
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:39,190
Number one, why would this occur?

42
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:40,790
Well, what happens is you get inflammation.

43
00:02:41,230 --> 00:02:42,610
Let's take this normal side here.

44
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:45,410
You get inflammation, and that leads to obstruction,

45
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:49,030
and then the material can't come out of this duct anymore,

46
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:51,230
and so you're going to get dilatation of that gland.

47
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:55,170
Now, of course, this is usually going to occur in a woman of reproductive age,

48
00:02:55,290 --> 00:02:58,810
and the reason it occurs in a woman of reproductive age is because

49
00:02:58,810 --> 00:03:05,490
often these are related to infection and sometimes sexually transmitted disease,

50
00:03:05,650 --> 00:03:11,470
and so that's the population that you would most likely see such an etiology occurring in.

51
00:03:11,830 --> 00:03:14,970
And so therefore, that's the classic patient that you would see this in.

52
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:17,470
And by the way, this is not an uncommon lesion to see clinically.

53
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:22,750
Now, the other thing that can occur is that these things can become further infected,

54
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:25,150
and you can actually develop an abscess here as well.

55
00:03:25,510 --> 00:03:27,590
But anyway, that would be a superimposed complication.

56
00:03:27,730 --> 00:03:31,630
And the idea here is that, remember the general pathology principle that I keep trying to nail home,

57
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:36,550
which is that any time you block a tube, it's not uncommon to get infection behind that block.

58
00:03:37,110 --> 00:03:42,450
So that's basically the Bartholin cyst, and it's important to know clinically because you'll see it,

59
00:03:42,450 --> 00:03:46,790
and it's certainly important to know for exams, and what they like to do is show you a picture

60
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:54,410
or tell you a description that there is a unilateral lesion in the lower vestibule adjacent

61
00:03:54,410 --> 00:03:58,630
to the vaginal canal, for example, and then you know that you're dealing with a Bartholin cyst.

62
00:03:58,810 --> 00:04:03,150
So let's read together what I've written for you in the book, and you can follow along there.

63
00:04:03,790 --> 00:04:06,990
So Bartholin cyst, it's a cystic dilatation of the Bartholin gland.

64
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:08,070
I've already explained that.

65
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:11,590
Again, it arises due to inflammation and obstruction of the gland.

66
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:12,510
We've done that.

67
00:04:12,750 --> 00:04:14,830
It usually occurs in women of reproductive age.

68
00:04:15,830 --> 00:04:20,070
The presentation is a unilateral painful cystic lesion, and where would that be?

69
00:04:20,150 --> 00:04:23,990
It would be exactly where the Bartholin duct is normally present,

70
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:27,370
which is at the lower vestibule adjacent to the vaginal canal.

71
00:04:27,710 --> 00:04:31,590
So basically, if this were to show up on an examination, it's an anatomy-type question.

72
00:04:31,590 --> 00:04:36,250
They're just asking you where is the Bartholin duct or gland normally located.

73
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:40,030
All right, so that's really the end of the Bartholin cyst.

74
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:45,090
Now, the next lesion we're going to go to is condyloma, and this is super high yield, of course,

75
00:04:45,270 --> 00:04:46,870
because these are relatively common.

76
00:04:47,750 --> 00:04:50,350
This is a warty neoplasm that will evolve our skin.

77
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:51,950
Often it can be very large.

78
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:57,150
And the most important point here is that it's most commonly due to HPV 6 or 11.

79
00:04:57,730 --> 00:05:02,570
Now, here I'd like to take a step back and remind you of a few very simple principles of HPV.

80
00:05:02,570 --> 00:05:08,610
I'm going to highlight these principles here in order to establish a very important framework

81
00:05:08,610 --> 00:05:13,370
that we're going to carry with us through the rest of our discussion of the female genital tract,

82
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:15,250
in particular the lower genital tract.

83
00:05:15,590 --> 00:05:21,290
So let me begin by reminding you that HPV can infect the lower genital tract.

84
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:28,830
Now, that's important because basically HPV is going to infect, for example, the vulva, the vaginal canal, and the cervix.

85
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:30,350
That's what I mean by the lower tract.

86
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:34,630
So when we talk about HPV infection, these are the three places where you classically see it.

87
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:41,390
You can see it, again, in the vulvar skin, or you can see it in the vaginal canal, or you can see it within the cervix.

88
00:05:41,470 --> 00:05:46,210
These are the three places in the lower tract, which really that is the definition of the lower tract.

89
00:05:46,650 --> 00:05:52,330
When there's an HPV infection, one of the key features is that there's going to be coilocytic change.

90
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:54,990
All right, so I'm just going to say coilo here.

91
00:05:54,990 --> 00:06:04,790
Now, coilocytic change is a change that we see histologically, and the way by which we identify that is you look at the nucleus of the cell, and it looks crinkled.

92
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:05,970
It looks like a raisin.

93
00:06:06,730 --> 00:06:11,810
And I'm going to show you a picture a little later, but I just want to sort of lay down some principles in your mind before we go forward.

94
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:18,430
So just, I would like you to know that, generally speaking, an HPV infection manifests itself histologically as coilocytic change.

95
00:06:18,970 --> 00:06:22,410
Now, you're well aware of the fact that HPV can be subdivided into two types.

96
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:26,870
It can be divided into a low-risk subtype and a high-risk subtype.

97
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:32,430
Important to know for examination purposes and for clinics what defines what's high-risk and low-risk.

98
00:06:32,510 --> 00:06:33,590
How do we make that determination?

99
00:06:33,590 --> 00:06:40,330
Basically, the question I'm asking you is, how do you know whether something is high-risk or low-risk?

100
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:43,450
Is it an antigen, or is there something that we measure, et cetera?

101
00:06:43,910 --> 00:06:47,050
And what you need to know is that it's based on DNA sequencing.

102
00:06:47,690 --> 00:06:53,970
It's the DNA sequencing that allows us to determine or subclassify HPV as high-risk or low-risk.

103
00:06:54,350 --> 00:06:59,010
And that helps to remind you that HPV is a DNA virus, and, of course, that's a very high-yield principle.

104
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:03,790
Now, basically, the low-risk subtypes, the key ones, are 6 and 11.

105
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:12,130
And the high-risk subtypes, the key ones, are 16, 18, 31, and 33.

106
00:07:12,850 --> 00:07:15,290
And those are the ones to be most familiar with.

107
00:07:15,410 --> 00:07:19,910
If you're limited on time and you're having trouble remembering this, the big ones are 16 and 18.

108
00:07:20,330 --> 00:07:21,850
But I'd like you to know that there's multiple.

109
00:07:22,050 --> 00:07:24,050
I'm just giving you the four most common here.

110
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:26,350
So the most common low-risks are 6 and 11.

111
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:28,850
The most common high-risk risks are 16 and 18.

112
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:32,930
Now, what I'd like to remind you of is that if a patient gets infected with a low-risk,

113
00:07:32,990 --> 00:07:34,250
and, by the way, what does low-risk mean?

114
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:38,870
Low-risk means low-risk to develop subsequent carcinoma.

115
00:07:39,730 --> 00:07:42,870
And high-risk means high-risk to develop subsequent carcinoma.

116
00:07:43,230 --> 00:07:48,690
Go back to our neoplasia chapter, and there you'll recall that we discussed that HPV is a virus

117
00:07:48,690 --> 00:07:50,930
that has the ability to induce carcinogenesis.

118
00:07:50,930 --> 00:07:55,250
And so low-risk means low-risk of carcinogenesis, i.e., development of cancer.

119
00:07:55,570 --> 00:07:58,790
High-risk means high-risk of carcinogenesis, i.e., development of cancer.

120
00:07:59,370 --> 00:08:03,470
Now, the low-risk, what I'd like to remind you of is it can infect, again, the vulva,

121
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:05,570
the vaginal canal, or the cervix.

122
00:08:05,830 --> 00:08:09,810
And the classic lesion that the low-risk is going to produce is the condyloma.

123
00:08:12,670 --> 00:08:17,790
And, of course, the condyloma is a warty neoplasm of squamous epithelium.

124
00:08:18,050 --> 00:08:18,330
All right?

125
00:08:18,330 --> 00:08:19,690
And so that's the condyloma.

126
00:08:20,350 --> 00:08:22,570
And it's going to be due to 6 or 11.

127
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:26,230
Now, again, if you get 6 or 11 in the vulva, it's a condyloma.

128
00:08:26,370 --> 00:08:28,890
If it's 6 or 11 in the vaginal canal, it's going to be a condyloma.

129
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:31,350
6 or 11 in the cervix can form a condyloma.

130
00:08:32,030 --> 00:08:36,270
And then the high-risk, the key problem with high-risk is that the patients,

131
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:38,690
when they get exposed to the virus, they'll get the infection.

132
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:40,550
And as they have the infection, it'll progress.

133
00:08:41,050 --> 00:08:46,810
And the main problem will be that the patients will get dysplasia over time.

134
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:50,930
And that dysplasia can eventually become carcinoma.

135
00:08:51,630 --> 00:08:51,770
All right?

136
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:56,550
So, for example, in the cervix, we call it CIN, the dysplasia.

137
00:08:56,990 --> 00:09:02,130
In the vagina, we call it VAIN, vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia.

138
00:09:02,210 --> 00:09:05,870
In the vulva, we call it vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia.

139
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:08,510
So these are the different types of dysplasia that can occur.

140
00:09:08,890 --> 00:09:12,330
And the carcinoma that's going to form is usually going to be a squamous cell carcinoma,

141
00:09:12,330 --> 00:09:16,530
but it doesn't necessarily have to be, as you'll see when we get to our discussion of cervix.

142
00:09:16,990 --> 00:09:20,970
Now, by the way, all of this is not necessarily written at this point in your text,

143
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:22,850
but it is written at a later point.

144
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:27,850
And so if you follow me here, I will, again, highlight many of these principles later.

145
00:09:28,270 --> 00:09:30,650
But I wanted to just lay down this general paradigm

146
00:09:30,650 --> 00:09:34,070
because it'll make our discussions a lot easier as we move forward.

147
00:09:34,530 --> 00:09:38,490
So now let's go back to the text, all right, and then read together.

148
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:40,150
So condyloma, what is it?

149
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:42,330
It's a warty neoplasma vulvar skin.

150
00:09:43,290 --> 00:09:47,990
It is often large, most commonly due to HPV 6 or 11,

151
00:09:48,570 --> 00:09:52,790
again, characterized by coelocytic change, and it rarely progresses to carcinoma.

152
00:09:53,110 --> 00:09:55,330
Now, I'd like to remind you, it's a low risk.

153
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:57,450
Low risk means there still is a risk.

154
00:09:57,490 --> 00:09:58,790
So it's not zero risk.

155
00:09:58,850 --> 00:10:01,790
It's a low risk, but it rarely progresses to carcinoma.

156
00:10:02,170 --> 00:10:04,850
This is a classic picture of the histology of a condyloma.

157
00:10:04,850 --> 00:10:08,290
Basically, this would be sort of the baseline of the epithelium.

158
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:11,450
And so this is the warty outgrowth of the cells.

159
00:10:11,830 --> 00:10:14,290
And if I were to show you a higher power of those cells,

160
00:10:14,570 --> 00:10:18,650
you would clearly see that this nucleus looks like it's kind of got this raisinoid appearance.

161
00:10:18,810 --> 00:10:20,330
Look at the, compare it to this,

162
00:10:20,410 --> 00:10:23,430
where this nucleus clearly has a very round nuclear envelope.

163
00:10:23,910 --> 00:10:29,790
And you look at this cell, and this cell's kind of got this raisin-looking nuclear envelope or nuclear shape.

164
00:10:29,790 --> 00:10:31,830
And so that's the coelocytic change.

165
00:10:32,150 --> 00:10:36,750
Very important to note that any time you get an HPV infection almost anywhere in the body,

166
00:10:36,850 --> 00:10:39,890
whether it be low risk or high risk, you're going to see coelocytic change.

167
00:10:40,970 --> 00:10:44,610
So that's my little summary of HPV and the lesions that it can cause.

168
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:47,630
And we're going to keep hitting on this as a recurrent theme.

169
00:10:48,130 --> 00:10:51,950
But for now, we're going to skip away from this and go to lichen sclerosis.

170
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:54,730
So lichen sclerosis, what this is,

171
00:10:54,730 --> 00:10:59,510
is this is a thinning of the epidermis and fibrosis of the underlying dermis.

172
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:03,530
Thinning of the epidermis and fibrosis of the underlying dermis.

173
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:06,050
It classically presents with leukoplakia.

174
00:11:06,170 --> 00:11:07,970
Leuco means white, and plakia means plaque.

175
00:11:08,050 --> 00:11:11,990
So the patients have a white patch on their skin in this region.

176
00:11:12,650 --> 00:11:16,170
And the classic description, for purposes of examinations especially,

177
00:11:16,330 --> 00:11:18,030
and even when you examine this clinically,

178
00:11:18,490 --> 00:11:21,250
is that it's parchment-like, super thin.

179
00:11:21,350 --> 00:11:23,210
The skin feels almost like paper.

180
00:11:23,690 --> 00:11:26,430
So you've got this paper-thin vulvar skin,

181
00:11:26,530 --> 00:11:28,470
and they call that parchment-like vulvar skin.

182
00:11:28,830 --> 00:11:31,050
This is usually seen in postmenopausal women.

183
00:11:31,210 --> 00:11:33,710
There's sort of atrophy that occurs of the epithelium.

184
00:11:34,190 --> 00:11:37,090
And then over time, there's some fibrosis of the dermis,

185
00:11:37,390 --> 00:11:40,010
and that results in what we call lichen sclerosis.

186
00:11:40,510 --> 00:11:42,210
Now, one very important principle here,

187
00:11:42,270 --> 00:11:43,670
and that is that this is benign.

188
00:11:44,710 --> 00:11:48,210
However, it is associated with a slightly increased risk

189
00:11:48,210 --> 00:11:49,450
of squamous cell carcinoma.

190
00:11:49,850 --> 00:11:51,510
You could probably bury that away for a minute,

191
00:11:51,670 --> 00:11:53,470
and I'll touch on it later as you'll see.

192
00:11:53,470 --> 00:11:58,350
Now, the other lesion to be familiar with when we talk about lichen sclerosis

193
00:11:58,350 --> 00:12:01,490
is sort of the opposite, and that's called lichen simplex chronicus.

194
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:03,810
And this is, instead of thinning of the skin,

195
00:12:03,870 --> 00:12:05,350
you actually get thickening of the skin.

196
00:12:05,810 --> 00:12:08,390
So that the patients have leukoplakia, which is a white patch,

197
00:12:08,650 --> 00:12:10,370
but it's actually, when you do an examination,

198
00:12:10,730 --> 00:12:14,890
you actually notice that there's thick, leather-like vulvar skin.

199
00:12:15,310 --> 00:12:17,950
And this is due to a hyperplasia of the squamous epithelium,

200
00:12:17,950 --> 00:12:21,850
and the reason you get hyperplasia is because there's chronic irritation and scratching.

201
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:26,530
This is benign with no increased risk for squamous cell carcinoma.

202
00:12:27,030 --> 00:12:30,730
So the way I think about this is sort of lichen sclerosis

203
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:33,550
represents thinning of the epidermis,

204
00:12:33,650 --> 00:12:35,350
usually in a postmenopausal woman,

205
00:12:36,230 --> 00:12:38,110
probably due to a little bit of atrophy

206
00:12:38,110 --> 00:12:41,270
and then some immune damage that occurs to the dermis.

207
00:12:41,810 --> 00:12:44,170
And lichen simplex chronicus, on the other hand,

208
00:12:44,170 --> 00:12:47,750
is hyperplasia due to chronic irritation with scratching

209
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:51,810
and that results in a thick, leather vulvar skin.

210
00:12:52,470 --> 00:12:55,930
So that's the lichen lesions,

211
00:12:56,090 --> 00:12:59,150
or the lichen sclerosis and the lichen simplex chronicus.

212
00:12:59,770 --> 00:13:02,670
Now the next discussion we come to is vulvar carcinoma.

213
00:13:03,110 --> 00:13:04,170
Now here's the idea here.

214
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:08,190
A cancer can arise within the vulva,

215
00:13:08,530 --> 00:13:11,890
and that is usually going to be from the squamous epithelium

216
00:13:11,890 --> 00:13:13,050
that lines the vulva.

217
00:13:13,310 --> 00:13:15,890
So you're going to get a squamous carcinoma, basically.

218
00:13:15,890 --> 00:13:17,530
This is relatively rare,

219
00:13:17,650 --> 00:13:20,930
and it accounts for only a very small percentage of female genital cancers.

220
00:13:21,550 --> 00:13:23,390
It's going to present as leukoplakia,

221
00:13:23,530 --> 00:13:26,150
and that's important because when it presents as leukoplakia,

222
00:13:26,410 --> 00:13:27,810
you now have to distinguish it, right?

223
00:13:27,850 --> 00:13:29,130
Because if you've got leukoplakia,

224
00:13:29,190 --> 00:13:29,830
then the question is,

225
00:13:29,910 --> 00:13:31,330
aha, is this lichen sclerosis?

226
00:13:31,470 --> 00:13:33,010
Is this lichen simplex chronicus?

227
00:13:33,070 --> 00:13:35,130
Or is this vulvar carcinoma, for example?

228
00:13:35,610 --> 00:13:37,030
So a biopsy will be performed.

229
00:13:37,030 --> 00:13:38,530
And when the biopsy is performed,

230
00:13:38,670 --> 00:13:41,190
that allows us to make that distinction.

231
00:13:41,830 --> 00:13:45,870
What's the most high yield in this discussion of vulvar carcinoma

232
00:13:45,870 --> 00:13:49,690
is to know that there are two pathways by which vulvar carcinoma can form.

233
00:13:50,070 --> 00:13:52,270
There's the HPV-related pathway.

234
00:13:52,850 --> 00:13:57,150
So in this particular case, what happens is the patient gets what type of HPV?

235
00:13:57,670 --> 00:13:58,830
High-risk HPV.

236
00:13:58,930 --> 00:14:00,430
And what are the high-risk HPV?

237
00:14:00,790 --> 00:14:05,350
16, 18, classically, or 31, 33, and then there are some others.

238
00:14:05,350 --> 00:14:08,010
But 16, 18 are the real major players here.

239
00:14:08,470 --> 00:14:10,290
So the patient gets high-risk HPV.

240
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:16,250
That eventually results in something called vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia,

241
00:14:16,430 --> 00:14:19,190
or VIN, which is sort of dysplasia.

242
00:14:19,590 --> 00:14:22,250
And then that dysplasia eventually becomes vulvar carcinoma.

243
00:14:22,590 --> 00:14:24,710
So that's pathway A, and that's HPV-related.

244
00:14:25,190 --> 00:14:27,530
Now the second pathway is called non-HPV-related.

245
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:33,150
And in non-HPV-related, the key sort of etiology or underlying cause

246
00:14:33,150 --> 00:14:35,710
is long-standing lichen sclerosis.

247
00:14:36,230 --> 00:14:39,170
Now remember, in lichen sclerosis, we have thinning of the epidermis

248
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:40,970
with sclerosis of the dermis.

249
00:14:41,030 --> 00:14:44,170
And I told you that it's benign, but that there was a slightly increased risk

250
00:14:44,170 --> 00:14:45,290
of squamous cell carcinoma.

251
00:14:46,150 --> 00:14:48,590
And the reason I made that point there was because I knew

252
00:14:48,590 --> 00:14:50,110
we were going to have this discussion here.

253
00:14:50,110 --> 00:14:54,390
And so when you think about the non-HPV-related causes of vulvar carcinoma,

254
00:14:54,590 --> 00:14:57,350
you really want to think about long-standing lichen sclerosis.

255
00:14:57,790 --> 00:15:00,350
Of course, that's going to be important because, for example,

256
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:06,110
if it's HPV-related, you'd expect to see it in a woman of 40 to 50 years of age.

257
00:15:06,490 --> 00:15:14,190
You know, she gets the HPV infection at around reproductive age, let's say 20, 25,

258
00:15:14,770 --> 00:15:17,590
and then it takes about 10 to 15 years to develop the dysplasia.

259
00:15:17,590 --> 00:15:21,330
So this would be seen, for example, in a woman of age 40 to 50.

260
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:26,810
Whereas the non-HPV-related, you know, that's a post-menopausal woman, much older,

261
00:15:27,290 --> 00:15:32,410
and so you're expecting to see that, for example, in a greater than 70-year-old female.

262
00:15:32,890 --> 00:15:34,610
All right, so again, what's the summary?

263
00:15:35,030 --> 00:15:37,630
It's that vulvar carcinoma has two pathways.

264
00:15:38,050 --> 00:15:42,050
Pathway A, HPV-related, which results in dysplasia,

265
00:15:42,470 --> 00:15:44,250
and then subsequently results in cancer.

266
00:15:44,250 --> 00:15:48,990
And then there's the non-HPV-related, which arises from lichen sclerosis.

267
00:15:49,410 --> 00:15:50,950
So let's walk through this in your text.

268
00:15:51,330 --> 00:15:52,510
So what do I say here?

269
00:15:52,870 --> 00:15:59,130
I say that in the HPV-related vulvar carcinoma, it's due to high-risk types, for example, 16 and 18,

270
00:15:59,550 --> 00:16:05,250
and it arises from vulvar intraepithelial neoplasia, which is really a dysplasia, also called VIN.

271
00:16:06,210 --> 00:16:10,410
Sort of like, just like you would call it in the cervix CIN, here we call it VIN.

272
00:16:10,410 --> 00:16:14,130
Now, there's also a non-HPV-related vulvar carcinoma.

273
00:16:14,350 --> 00:16:17,230
This arises from long-standing lichen sclerosis.

274
00:16:17,970 --> 00:16:22,770
And basically, the patients have chronic inflammation and irritation from that lichen sclerosis,

275
00:16:22,890 --> 00:16:24,650
which eventually leads to the carcinoma.

276
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:29,150
And again, this is usually going to be seen in the elderly women greater than the age of 70.

277
00:16:29,650 --> 00:16:32,270
So that's our discussion of vulvar carcinoma.

278
00:16:32,270 --> 00:16:37,870
Now, the next very high-yield topic that we want to discuss is extramammary Paget's disease.

279
00:16:37,990 --> 00:16:40,590
And this is really the final vulvar lesion that we're going to talk about.

280
00:16:41,030 --> 00:16:44,790
Now, I've highlighted these principles in your book, but I want to explain it before we go any further.

281
00:16:45,770 --> 00:16:50,070
So Paget, as I like to say, for the students who have seen me speak about this,

282
00:16:50,530 --> 00:16:51,950
Paget was a very busy man.

283
00:16:52,070 --> 00:16:53,290
He discovered many things.

284
00:16:53,290 --> 00:16:57,830
You know, you've got Paget's disease of the nipple, you have extramammary Paget's disease of the vulva,

285
00:16:57,930 --> 00:17:00,010
you've got Paget's disease of the bone, etc.

286
00:17:00,010 --> 00:17:05,930
Now, in extramammary Paget's disease, and whether you talk about extramammary or mammary,

287
00:17:06,010 --> 00:17:08,730
why don't we just say when you have Paget's disease of this skin,

288
00:17:08,830 --> 00:17:11,270
for example, whether it be of the nipple or of the vulva,

289
00:17:11,670 --> 00:17:17,090
the key problem is that you've got this malignant epithelial cells that are present in the epidermis.

290
00:17:17,690 --> 00:17:20,330
So that's really the defining feature of the disease.

291
00:17:20,510 --> 00:17:25,470
You've got the epidermal skin, and then you've got these foreign cells within there that are malignant,

292
00:17:25,690 --> 00:17:29,410
that are sort of walking their way or present within the skin itself.

293
00:17:29,410 --> 00:17:31,010
And that's Paget's disease.

294
00:17:31,190 --> 00:17:35,330
Now, you can see that in the nipple, and that would be called Paget's disease of the nipple.

295
00:17:35,590 --> 00:17:40,250
And you can see that in the skin of the vulva, and that's called Paget's disease of the vulva.

296
00:17:41,110 --> 00:17:43,970
When it presents, whether it be in the nipple or in the vulva,

297
00:17:43,970 --> 00:17:51,150
it presents as an erythematous, itchy, ulcerated skin.

298
00:17:51,150 --> 00:17:58,990
And again, it's going to be due to the fact that there are malignant epithelial cells within the epidermis of the skin.

299
00:17:59,490 --> 00:18:02,290
Here's what it looks like if we were to just look at it on histology.

300
00:18:02,550 --> 00:18:03,830
This is clearly skin.

301
00:18:04,070 --> 00:18:07,050
Here we have epidermis, everything above this basement membrane.

302
00:18:07,310 --> 00:18:09,090
All of that represents epidermis.

303
00:18:09,370 --> 00:18:13,710
And you can see that there's these extra cells present within here that don't belong.

304
00:18:13,710 --> 00:18:17,570
And so this represents a malignant population of cells,

305
00:18:17,730 --> 00:18:21,270
and these malignant cells are going to be indicative of Paget's disease.

306
00:18:21,490 --> 00:18:25,270
And in this case, because it's the vulva, we call it extramammary Paget's disease.

307
00:18:25,970 --> 00:18:32,570
Now, what's very important, and I would say the highest yield point about this disease and this location,

308
00:18:32,870 --> 00:18:40,950
is that when you have extramammary Paget's disease, the differential diagnosis is carcinoma versus melanoma.

309
00:18:40,950 --> 00:18:44,730
Now, why would we think about melanoma here?

310
00:18:44,810 --> 00:18:48,210
Well, remember that in melanoma, we're going to have a malignant population of cells

311
00:18:48,210 --> 00:18:52,410
that would also be present within the epidermis and could also invade down into the dermis,

312
00:18:52,510 --> 00:18:57,090
but begins as a malignant population of cells within the epidermis.

313
00:18:57,450 --> 00:19:00,870
And melanoma can also not uncommonly occur in the vulva.

314
00:19:00,970 --> 00:19:03,270
One of the locations of melanoma is the vulva.

315
00:19:03,630 --> 00:19:06,890
So we have to make the distinction here and ask a very simple question.

316
00:19:06,890 --> 00:19:13,370
Is this carcinoma, i.e., is it extramammary Paget's disease, or is it melanoma?

317
00:19:13,690 --> 00:19:17,470
And the way by which we can make that distinction is by doing stains.

318
00:19:17,610 --> 00:19:19,630
And so the examiners love to play with these stains.

319
00:19:19,750 --> 00:19:22,010
And by the way, we do this clinically all the time as well.

320
00:19:22,070 --> 00:19:26,930
If I had a case come across my microscope, I would have to do stains to ensure what I'm dealing with.

321
00:19:27,350 --> 00:19:31,170
Now, of course, Paget's disease, extramammary Paget's disease, is a carcinoma.

322
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:32,910
So it would be keratin-positive.

323
00:19:33,250 --> 00:19:38,990
Go back to our discussion in the neoplasia lecture and recall that keratin is an intermediate filament

324
00:19:38,990 --> 00:19:41,670
that would be present in epithelial cells.

325
00:19:42,270 --> 00:19:44,930
It's the intermediate filament present in epithelial cells.

326
00:19:45,410 --> 00:19:49,930
Therefore, if keratin is present within the cell, it must be epithelial.

327
00:19:50,030 --> 00:19:53,090
And if it's epithelial and malignant, then we call that carcinoma.

328
00:19:53,590 --> 00:19:56,310
So it's very important to note that we wouldn't see keratin in melanoma.

329
00:19:56,410 --> 00:19:57,930
We wouldn't see keratin in lymphoma.

330
00:19:57,930 --> 00:20:00,670
We wouldn't see keratin in sarcoma, classically speaking.

331
00:20:01,170 --> 00:20:04,810
And so if we've got keratin-positivity, that indicates that we have a carcinoma,

332
00:20:04,930 --> 00:20:06,850
and that's classic for Paget's disease.

333
00:20:07,450 --> 00:20:11,570
Now, another important thing to note is that the cells would be S100-negative.

334
00:20:11,910 --> 00:20:12,690
Why is that important?

335
00:20:12,830 --> 00:20:15,570
Because melanoma is classically S100-positive.

336
00:20:15,930 --> 00:20:19,190
So the defining stain here for melanoma would be S100.

337
00:20:20,070 --> 00:20:22,090
Of course, melanoma would be keratin-negative,

338
00:20:22,310 --> 00:20:25,150
and, of course, Paget's disease would be S100-negative.

339
00:20:25,750 --> 00:20:29,890
Now, the last stain you'd have to be familiar with is something called PAS.

340
00:20:29,890 --> 00:20:33,530
PAS is a stain that has the ability to mark mucus.

341
00:20:33,930 --> 00:20:37,750
And if a cell is producing mucus, then it must be carcinoma.

342
00:20:37,870 --> 00:20:41,330
It must be epithelial, because only epithelial cells make mucus.

343
00:20:41,730 --> 00:20:45,250
So if it was PAS-positive, then we would call it Paget's disease.

344
00:20:45,390 --> 00:20:48,790
And if it was PAS-negative, it would be a hint that we're dealing with melanoma.

345
00:20:49,130 --> 00:20:52,870
But to be honest with you, this is really the way examiners like to go after this,

346
00:20:52,870 --> 00:20:56,590
and this is really the way that we also clinically make this distinction.

347
00:20:56,750 --> 00:20:58,210
We do a keratin and an S100.

348
00:20:58,450 --> 00:21:01,590
Keratin-positive, Paget's disease, it's carcinoma.

349
00:21:01,930 --> 00:21:06,150
S100-positive, melanoma, highly suggestive of melanoma.

350
00:21:06,570 --> 00:21:08,570
So this is a very important point.

351
00:21:08,910 --> 00:21:10,870
Now, I'd like to make one last point, and then we'll be done.

352
00:21:11,130 --> 00:21:12,910
And that is we've got to make the distinction.

353
00:21:13,530 --> 00:21:18,870
I've sort of used Paget's disease as a general term when thinking about the nipple and the vulva.

354
00:21:19,190 --> 00:21:21,410
But I'd like to make one very important distinction.

355
00:21:21,870 --> 00:21:24,510
And that is when a patient has Paget's disease of the nipple,

356
00:21:24,850 --> 00:21:27,610
it means that there is a cancer someplace in the breast.

357
00:21:28,050 --> 00:21:31,570
There will usually, almost always, be a cancer someplace in the breast.

358
00:21:31,650 --> 00:21:36,570
But when a patient has Paget's disease of the vulva, there usually is no underlying cancer.

359
00:21:37,550 --> 00:21:40,190
And that is a very important high-yield distinction to be made.

360
00:21:40,190 --> 00:21:43,250
So the malignant cells are just within the epidermis,

361
00:21:43,430 --> 00:21:48,770
and there is usually nothing underneath the epidermis in the deeper tissues.

362
00:21:49,250 --> 00:21:51,590
And so, again, that's one very high-yield point to be made.

363
00:21:51,850 --> 00:21:54,270
That is the end of our discussion of vulva.

364
00:21:54,350 --> 00:21:59,370
So we'll stop there, and then we'll move on to the vaginal canal in the next section.

